{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.HalfPlaneNonCancellation","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.HalfPlaneNonCancellation","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Complex.Arg","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.BigOperators"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.cos_mul_sum_norm_le_norm_sum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.cos_mul_sum_norm_le_norm_sum","kind":"theorem","line":106,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/HalfPlaneNonCancellation.lean#L106-L150","doc":"**Terms in a half-plane keep a `cos α` share of their mass.**\n\nIf, after rotating by `e^{-iθ}`, every term has argument within `α ≤ π/2` of the real\naxis — i.e. the terms lie in a closed cone of half-angle `α` about direction `θ` — then\n\n    `cos α · Σ‖zᵢ‖ ≤ ‖Σ zᵢ‖`.\n\nAt `α = π/2` this degenerates to `0 ≤ ‖Σ zᵢ‖`, which is the honest boundary: a closed\nhalf-plane permits `1 + (−1) · i·i`-style cancellation only in the limit, and the bound\nmust vanish there. Strictly inside, the constant is strictly positive and *independent\nof the number of terms* — which is what makes it useful against a sum whose length grows\nwith `N`.\n\nZero terms are allowed and need no hypothesis: `arg 0 = 0`, so they satisfy the bound\nautomatically. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.norm_sum_ge_of_rotated_re","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.norm_sum_ge_of_rotated_re","kind":"theorem","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/HalfPlaneNonCancellation.lean#L84-L104","doc":"**No cancellation after a rotation, in terms of real parts.**\n\nIf some unit `u` rotates every term so that its real part is at least `c` times its\nnorm, then the sum keeps at least the fraction `c` of the total mass.\n\nThis is the whole content; the arg-based statement below is a repackaging. The proof is\nthree steps — sum the pointwise bound, pull the rotation out of the sum, and use\n`Re w ≤ ‖w‖`, which is the reverse-direction triangle inequality that `norm_sum_le` does\nnot provide.\n\n`c` is unconstrained: it may be negative, in which case the conclusion is weak but true,\nand `c = 0` recovers the trivial bound. ","signatureChunk":0}]}